LIB_LOAD("cs",{"cs1":{"challenge":[{"q":"In which village was Charles Spurgeon born?","o":["Kelvedon","Stambourne","Colchester","London"],"why":"In Essex, in 1834."},{"q":"Who did Charles live with as a little boy?","o":["His grandparents","His uncle in London","A teacher","Missionaries"],"why":"At Stambourne."},{"q":"Who looked after Charles like a big sister at the manse?","o":["Aunt Ann","Aunt Mary","His mother","Mrs Knill"],"why":"His father's young sister."},{"q":"Which famous book did Charles find in the room of books?","o":["The Pilgrim's Progress","The Holy War","Quo Vadis","Robinson Crusoe"],"why":"He read it a hundred times."},{"q":"What did little Charles say to Old Roads?","o":["What are you doing here, Elijah?","Buy me a sweet!","Good morning!","Can I have a drink?"],"why":"And Old Roads changed."},{"q":"Where had Mr Knill worked as a missionary?","o":["India and Russia","Nigeria and Ghana","China and Japan","Brazil and Peru"],"why":"A famous visitor at Stambourne."},{"q":"What did Mr Knill give Charles?","o":["A sixpence for learning a hymn","A Bible","A horse","A book"],"why":"'God moves in a mysterious way'."},{"q":"What was Charles's father's other role?","o":["He preached on Sundays","He was a sailor","He was a soldier","He was a king"],"why":"He was often away preaching."},{"q":"How did Charles's mother pray for her children?","o":["By name, with her arms around them","Silently in another room","Only at Christmas","She didn't"],"why":"Sometimes with tears."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Say not, I am a ___.\"","o":["child","king","fool","sinner"],"why":"Jeremiah 1:7."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! How many years did Grandfather James Spurgeon preach at Stambourne?","o":["More than fifty","Five","Ten","One"],"why":"A lifetime of faithful preaching."},"choices":[{"title":"The book you choose","q":"You have some free time and two choices: a gripping Christian adventure book, or scrolling through videos you've seen before.","o":[{"t":"Pick up the book. What you read shapes who you become.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Charles read The Pilgrim's Progress a hundred times, and it shaped his whole life."},{"t":"Scroll. Reading is for school.","f":"Reading for fun can feed your heart and imagination."},{"t":"Do neither and complain you're bored.","f":"Boredom can be the start of a great adventure in a book!"}]},{"title":"Speaking up respectfully","q":"Your older cousin, who says he's a Christian, keeps posting bad language online. Your aunt is upset about it.","o":[{"t":"Message him privately and kindly: \"I care about you. This doesn't fit who you are.\"","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Like little Charles, speak the truth in love, and respectfully."},{"t":"Mock him publicly in the comments.","f":"That's shaming, not helping."},{"t":"Copy his language.","f":"That just spreads the problem."}]},{"title":"An encourager","q":"A younger child at church tells you he wants to be a pastor when he grows up. Others laugh.","o":[{"t":"Encourage him: \"God could really use you! Let's pray about it.\"","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mr Knill encouraged little Charles, and look what happened."},{"t":"Laugh along.","f":"You never know what God will do with a child."},{"t":"Tell him it's too hard.","f":"Say not, I am a child."}]},{"title":"Family prayer","q":"Your mum asks everyone to pray together after dinner, but you'd rather go and play.","o":[{"t":"Join in gladly, and maybe even pray for each person by name.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Charles never forgot his mother's prayers."},{"t":"Join, but roll your eyes.","f":"Those moments matter more than you know."},{"t":"Pretend to be asleep.","f":"Don't miss what God might do in those minutes."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"stambourne","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 1 of Spurgeon's story in real life."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"🌱 Learn the Bible now. Childhood seeds grow into great things."},{"sc":"study","n":"📚 Choose good books, and read your favourites again and again."},{"sc":"inn","n":"🗣️ Speak the truth in love, bravely and respectfully."},{"sc":"arbour","n":"🪙 Encourage younger children. God may have big plans for them."},{"sc":"home","n":"🙏 Value the people who pray for you, and start praying for others."}],"stops":{"cs1s1":{"story":[{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Charles Haddon {Spurgeon} was born on 19 June 1834, in the village of {Kelvedon} in Essex, England."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Many years later, he became one of the most famous preachers who ever lived. People called him 'the Prince of Preachers'. Millions read his sermons. But he started life as an ordinary little boy."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"When Charles was still a toddler, his parents sent him to live with his grandparents in the little village of {Stambourne}. His family was growing, and money was short."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"His grandfather, James Spurgeon, was the minister of the village chapel, called the {Meeting House}. He preached there for more than fifty years."},{"sc":"stambourne","w":"grandspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"Come, Charles. Sit with me while I study. One day you'll love these books as I do."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"The old {manse}, the minister's house, was big and rambling, with dark corners, creaky stairs and a garden full of trees."},{"sc":"stambourne","w":"auntann","e":"big","t":"Charles! Dinner! And wash those muddy hands first!"},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"His Aunt Ann, his father's young sister, still lived at home. She spoiled him, played with him and looked after him like a big sister."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Little Charles sat in the Meeting House every Sunday, listening to his grandfather preach. He didn't understand everything. But the Bible was sinking into him, week after week."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Charles lived at Stambourne until he was about six. After that, he came back every summer holiday. He always called it one of the happiest places on earth."}],"quote":{"text":"And that from a child thou hast known the holy scriptures, which are able to make thee wise unto salvation through faith which is in Christ Jesus.","who":"2 Timothy 3:15","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Seeds in childhood","text":"Charles grew up hearing the Bible from his grandfather every Sunday, before he understood much of it. What you learn as a child sinks deep. The Bible stories and verses you learn now are seeds God can grow later."},"think":"Who are the 'grandfathers' and 'Aunt Anns' in your life: older people who teach you about God and care for you?","quiz":[{"q":"When was Charles Spurgeon born?","o":["19 June 1834","6 January 1850","1 January 1900","25 December 1805"],"why":"In Kelvedon, Essex."},{"q":"What were people later to call him?","o":["The Prince of Preachers","The Pilgrim","The Mill Boy","The Bishop"],"why":"Millions read his sermons."},{"q":"Where did Charles live as a little boy?","o":["With his grandparents at Stambourne","In London","In Scotland","On a ship"],"why":"Money was short at home."},{"q":"What did his grandfather do?","o":["Preached at the Meeting House for more than fifty years","Ran a farm","Sold books","Was a soldier"],"why":"James Spurgeon."},{"q":"What does 2 Timothy 3:15 say about the scriptures?","o":["They are able to make you wise unto salvation","They are only for adults","They are too hard","They don't matter"],"why":"From a child."}]},"cs1s2":{"story":[{"sc":"study","n":"In the old manse there was a small, dim room that hardly anyone used. One of its windows had been bricked up long ago."},{"sc":"study","n":"But to little Charles, it was a treasure cave. It was full of old books: big heavy ones, with pictures."},{"sc":"study","w":"charlieboy","e":"big","t":"Aunt Ann! There's a book here with a picture of a man with a big burden on his back!"},{"sc":"study","n":"That book was The Pilgrim's Progress by John Bunyan. Yes, the same story at the start of this library!"},{"sc":"study","n":"Charles pored over the pictures of Christian, the Slough of Despond, Apollyon and the Celestial City. As soon as he could read, he read it again and again."},{"sc":"study","n":"He also found {Foxe's Book of Martyrs}, full of stories of brave Christians who would not give up their faith, even when threatened with death."},{"sc":"study","w":"charlieboy","e":"worried","t":"They were so brave. Would I be brave like that?"},{"sc":"study","n":"Years later, Charles wrote that next to the Bible, The Pilgrim's Progress was the book he loved most. He believed he had read it through at least a hundred times!"},{"sc":"study","n":"Those books shaped his imagination. When he became a preacher, he filled his sermons with stories and pictures, just like the ones he'd loved as a boy in that little room."}],"quote":{"text":"Next to the Bible, the book that I value most is John Bunyan's Pilgrim's Progress. I believe I have read it through at least a hundred times.","who":"C. H. Spurgeon","tag":"Spurgeon's words"},"lesson":{"title":"What you read shapes you","text":"Charles read The Pilgrim's Progress about a hundred times, and it shaped his whole life. The books you read, the shows you watch and the stories you love are shaping who you become. Choose them well!"},"think":"Charles read his favourite book a hundred times. Which book or story could you read again and again to grow your faith?","quiz":[{"q":"What was special about the little room in the manse?","o":["It was full of old books","It had a piano","It was the kitchen","It was full of toys"],"why":"A treasure cave."},{"q":"Which book did Charles love next to the Bible?","o":["The Pilgrim's Progress","Robinson Crusoe","A dictionary","A cookbook"],"why":"By John Bunyan."},{"q":"About how many times did he read it?","o":["At least a hundred","Once","Twice","Never"],"why":"Again and again."},{"q":"What was Foxe's Book of Martyrs about?","o":["Brave Christians who would not give up their faith","Cooking","Animals","Football"],"why":"It made Charles wonder if he would be brave."},{"q":"How did those books shape his preaching?","o":["He filled sermons with stories and pictures","He stopped reading","He preached only in Latin","They didn't"],"why":"What you read shapes you."}]},"cs1s3":{"story":[{"sc":"stambourne","n":"In Stambourne there was a man everybody called Old Roads. He was a member of Grandfather's church."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"But Old Roads had a bad habit. He spent a lot of time at the village pub, drinking beer and smoking with the rough men there."},{"sc":"stambourne","w":"grandspurgeon","e":"worried","t":"Poor Roads. He's breaking my heart. He belongs to our church, but he lives as if he doesn't know God at all."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Little Charles heard his grandfather sigh. He was only about six years old. But he decided to do something about it."},{"sc":"inn","n":"One day, he marched right into the pub. There sat Old Roads with his beer and his pipe."},{"sc":"inn","w":"charlieboy","e":"stern","t":"What are you doing here, Elijah? Sitting with the ungodly, and you a member of a church, breaking your pastor's heart! I'm ashamed of you!"},{"sc":"inn","n":"The tiny boy borrowed his words from the Bible, where God asked Elijah, 'What doest thou here?' Then he turned around and walked out."},{"sc":"inn","n":"Old Roads was stunned. Nobody had ever spoken to him like that, let alone a six-year-old."},{"sc":"stambourne","w":"oldroads","e":"worried","t":"Out of the mouths of babes. That child's right. I've been a fool."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Old Roads put down his beer and went straight to the minister to say sorry. From that day on, he lived a changed life, and became one of the most loyal members of the church."}],"quote":{"text":"Faithful are the wounds of a friend.","who":"Proverbs 27:6","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Brave and honest","text":"A six-year-old spoke the truth to a grown man, because he cared about him and his grandfather. Speaking up takes courage, and we should always do it respectfully. But an honest word given in love can turn someone's life around."},"think":"Little Charles spoke boldly. How can young people speak the truth to others in a way that is brave but still respectful and loving?","quiz":[{"q":"What was Old Roads's bad habit?","o":["Drinking and smoking at the pub","Stealing","Lying","Skipping meals"],"why":"Though he was a church member."},{"q":"How old was Charles when he spoke to Old Roads?","o":["About six","About sixteen","About thirty","About two"],"why":"A tiny boy!"},{"q":"What Bible character did Charles call Old Roads?","o":["Elijah","Moses","Goliath","Pharaoh"],"why":"'What doest thou here, Elijah?'"},{"q":"What did Old Roads do afterwards?","o":["Said sorry to the minister and changed his life","Got angry","Moved away","Drank more"],"why":"Out of the mouths of babes."},{"q":"What does Proverbs 27:6 say?","o":["Faithful are the wounds of a friend","Never correct anyone","Friends should only flatter","Enemies are honest"],"why":"A loving, honest word."}]},"cs1s4":{"story":[{"sc":"stambourne","n":"When Charles was ten, he was back at Stambourne for the summer. A famous visitor came to stay: Mr Richard Knill, a missionary who had worked in India and Russia."},{"sc":"arbour","n":"Early each morning, Mr Knill took Charles out into the garden. In a quiet arbour under the yew trees, he talked to him about Jesus and prayed with him."},{"sc":"arbour","w":"knill","e":"smile","t":"Charles, do you love Jesus? Let's pray together that you will know him for yourself."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"On the last morning before he left, Mr Knill did something strange. In front of the whole family, he took Charles on his knee and made a prediction."},{"sc":"stambourne","w":"knill","e":"big","t":"This child will one day preach the gospel to thousands, and God has great blessings in store for him."},{"sc":"stambourne","w":"knill","e":"smile","t":"Charles, promise me this: when you preach in {Rowland Hill's chapel}, you will have them sing the hymn, 'God moves in a mysterious way, His wonders to perform.'"},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Then he gave Charles a {sixpence} as a reward for learning the hymn by heart."},{"sc":"stambourne","w":"charlieboy","e":"shock","t":"Me? Preach to thousands? But I'm just a boy!"},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"It seemed impossible. But many years later, Charles did preach at Rowland Hill's chapel, and at Rowland Hill's other chapel in the countryside. And each time, just as he had promised, the congregation sang 'God moves in a mysterious way'."},{"sc":"stambourne","n":"Spurgeon said he could hardly believe how exactly it came true. A kind man had taken time to pray with a small boy, and God remembered."}],"quote":{"text":"God moves in a mysterious way, His wonders to perform; He plants His footsteps in the sea, and rides upon the storm.","who":"William Cowper's hymn (1773), sung as Spurgeon had promised","tag":"An old hymn"},"lesson":{"title":"Take time for children","text":"Mr Knill was a famous missionary, but he got up early to pray with a ten-year-old boy. Never underestimate what God might do with a young person. And never think you're too young for God to have great plans for you."},"think":"Mr Knill saw something in Charles that nobody else noticed. Who has encouraged you about your future? Who could you encourage?","quiz":[{"q":"Who was Mr Richard Knill?","o":["A missionary who had worked in India and Russia","Charles's uncle","A shopkeeper","A soldier"],"why":"A famous visitor."},{"q":"Where did Mr Knill pray with Charles?","o":["In an arbour in the garden","In a pub","On a ship","In London"],"why":"Early each morning."},{"q":"What did Mr Knill predict?","o":["Charles would preach the gospel to thousands","Charles would become a king","Charles would be rich","Charles would be a sailor"],"why":"It seemed impossible."},{"q":"Which hymn did Charles promise would be sung?","o":["God moves in a mysterious way","Amazing Grace","Happy Birthday","Rock of Ages"],"why":"When he preached in Rowland Hill's chapel."},{"q":"Did the prediction come true?","o":["Yes, exactly as promised","No","Only partly","Nobody knows"],"why":"God remembered."}]},"cs1s5":{"story":[{"sc":"home","n":"When Charles was about six, he went back to live with his parents in {Colchester}. His father, John, was a businessman and also preached on Sundays in a village chapel."},{"sc":"home","n":"Because his father was often away preaching, his mother, Eliza, gathered the children around the table on Sunday evenings."},{"sc":"home","w":"elizaspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"Come, children. Let's read the Bible together, and then we'll pray."},{"sc":"home","n":"She read a passage, explained it simply, and then she prayed for each of her children by name, with her arms around them."},{"sc":"home","n":"Sometimes she prayed so earnestly that she wept. Charles never forgot one prayer in particular."},{"sc":"home","w":"elizaspurgeon","e":"worried","t":"Lord, if my children go on in their sins, it won't be because they didn't know. They have heard the truth. Please, save them."},{"sc":"home","n":"Those words went deep into Charles's heart. Years later, he said that his mother's prayers and tears did more to lead him to Christ than almost anything else."},{"sc":"home","n":"Like Timothy in the Bible, whose faith first lived in his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice, Charles had a home full of faith. But he still had to come to Jesus himself. And that wouldn't be easy."}],"quote":{"text":"Her children arise up, and call her blessed.","who":"Proverbs 31:28","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Praying homes","text":"Eliza Spurgeon's Sunday evening prayers shaped the boy who became the Prince of Preachers. Prayer at home matters enormously. If someone prays for you, thank them. And one day, you can be the one who prays for others."},"think":"Who prays for you by name? Have you ever thanked them? How might you pray for someone in your family this week?","quiz":[{"q":"Where did Charles's parents live?","o":["Colchester","London","Stambourne","Cambridge"],"why":"He went back to them at about six."},{"q":"What did his mother do on Sunday evenings?","o":["Read the Bible and prayed with the children","Went out shopping","Watched shows","Slept"],"why":"With her arms around them."},{"q":"What did she pray for?","o":["That her children would be saved","To be rich","For good weather","For a new house"],"why":"They had heard the truth."},{"q":"What did Charles later say about his mother's prayers?","o":["They did a great deal to lead him to Christ","They were boring","He forgot them","They embarrassed him"],"why":"He never forgot."},{"q":"Which Bible verse speaks of children calling their mother blessed?","o":["Proverbs 31:28","Genesis 1:1","John 3:16","Psalm 23:1"],"why":"Her children arise up."}]}}},"cs2":{"challenge":[{"q":"For about how many years did Charles struggle without peace?","o":["About five","One","Twenty","Fifty"],"why":"From about age ten."},{"q":"What did Charles feel like, from his favourite book?","o":["Christian with the burden on his back","Giant Despair","Mr Worldly Wiseman","Evangelist"],"why":"Weighed down."},{"q":"What mistake was Charles making?","o":["Trying to save himself by his own effort","Not reading enough","Eating too much","Sleeping too long"],"why":"Salvation is a gift."},{"q":"Where did Charles study at fourteen?","o":["An agricultural college in Maidstone","Oxford","London University","Cambridge University"],"why":"For a year."},{"q":"What was Charles's job at Newmarket?","o":["Assistant teacher","Cook","Gardener","Preacher"],"why":"At fifteen."},{"q":"What did Mary King teach Charles?","o":["Theology, especially God's grace","Cooking","Riding","Singing"],"why":"In the school kitchen."},{"q":"Why did Charles visit chapel after chapel?","o":["To find out how a sinner could be saved","To hear music","To meet friends","For warmth"],"why":"He was desperate for peace."},{"q":"On what street was the little chapel?","o":["Artillery Street","Baker Street","Wilson Street","Ear-gate Street"],"why":"In Colchester."},{"q":"Where did Charles sit in the chapel?","o":["Under the gallery, near the back","In the pulpit","Outside","At the front"],"why":"Waiting for the preacher."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Seek, and ye shall ___.\"","o":["find","lose","wait","hide"],"why":"Matthew 7:7."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did people say about how the Primitive Methodists sang?","o":["So loudly it gave you a headache","Very quietly","Only in Latin","They never sang"],"why":"Charles went in anyway!"},"choices":[{"title":"All the answers, no peace","q":"You know all the Bible stories and always win Bible quizzes. But secretly, you don't feel close to God at all.","o":[{"t":"Be honest with God about it, and talk to a trusted Christian adult.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Charles knew all the answers too, but needed to come to Jesus himself."},{"t":"Keep it hidden. People think you're a great Christian.","f":"God wants your heart, not just your answers."},{"t":"Decide faith isn't for you.","f":"Charles's searching led him to Jesus. Keep seeking."}]},{"title":"Learning from the cook","q":"The elderly security guard at your school always hums hymns and once shared a Bible verse with you. Your friends ignore him.","o":[{"t":"Greet him, ask about his faith, and listen. You might learn a lot.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Spurgeon learned deep truths from a school cook."},{"t":"Ignore him like everyone else.","f":"You might miss a Mary King."},{"t":"Laugh at his humming.","f":"Respect everyone, whatever their job."}]},{"title":"Plans snowed in","q":"Your family's trip to visit cousins is cancelled because of a flood on the road. You're really disappointed.","o":[{"t":"Feel disappointed, then look for what God might do with the day.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! A snowstorm led Charles to the most important morning of his life."},{"t":"Sulk all day.","f":"You might miss something good."},{"t":"Blame your parents.","f":"It's not their fault, and God can use it."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"night","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 2 of Spurgeon's story in real life."},{"sc":"home","n":"💡 Don't just know about God. Come to know him yourself."},{"sc":"college","n":"🎒 Love learning, but remember that only Jesus can save."},{"sc":"kitchen","n":"🍲 Learn from ordinary people who know God well."},{"sc":"church","n":"🔍 Keep seeking. And when you know the way, tell it plainly."},{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"❄️ When your plans get snowed in, God may be leading you somewhere better."}],"stops":{"cs2s1":{"story":[{"sc":"home","n":"Charles grew up knowing more about the Bible than almost any boy in England. He could quote whole chapters. He knew all the right answers."},{"sc":"home","n":"But there was a problem. He knew about God, but he didn't know God. And deep inside, he wasn't at peace."},{"sc":"night","n":"From about the age of ten, Charles began to feel a heavy {conviction}: a sense of how sinful his heart was before a holy God."},{"sc":"night","w":"charlieteen","e":"worried","t":"I try to be good. I pray. I read the Bible. But I can't make my heart clean. I feel like Christian with the burden on his back."},{"sc":"night","n":"Just like the pilgrim in his favourite book, Charles felt weighed down. He wrote later that he was miserable, for years."},{"sc":"night","n":"He tried harder to be good. He made promises. He prayed long prayers. Nothing worked. The more he tried, the worse he felt."},{"sc":"night","n":"Do you see what was happening? Charles was trying to save himself by his own effort. He hadn't yet understood that salvation is a gift you receive, not a prize you earn."},{"sc":"night","n":"These years of struggle lasted about five years. They were dark. But later, they helped him understand and comfort people who felt exactly the same way."}],"quote":{"text":"For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God.","who":"Romans 3:23","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Knowing about vs knowing","text":"Charles knew all the Bible answers, but he didn't yet know God personally, and he had no peace. You can know a lot about Jesus and still not trust him. Faith is not just knowing facts. It's coming to him yourself."},"think":"What's the difference between knowing ABOUT someone and actually knowing them? How is that like knowing God?","quiz":[{"q":"What did Charles know a lot about?","o":["The Bible","Football","Horses","Cooking"],"why":"He could quote whole chapters."},{"q":"What was Charles missing?","o":["Peace with God","Books","Friends","Food"],"why":"He knew about God, but didn't know God."},{"q":"What is conviction?","o":["A deep sense of having done wrong before God","A prize","A kind of school","A happy feeling"],"why":"It weighed Charles down."},{"q":"Why didn't Charles's efforts work?","o":["He was trying to save himself instead of receiving God's gift","He didn't try","He was too young","He prayed too much"],"why":"Salvation is a gift."},{"q":"What does Romans 3:23 say?","o":["All have sinned and come short of God's glory","Some people never sin","Only adults sin","Sin doesn't matter"],"why":"Everyone needs a Saviour."}]},"cs2s2":{"story":[{"sc":"college","n":"Charles was very clever. At school in Colchester, he often came top of his class, especially in English and maths."},{"sc":"college","w":"mrleeding","e":"smile","t":"Spurgeon, you have a remarkable memory. And you read faster than anyone I've ever taught."},{"sc":"college","n":"His teacher, Mr Leeding, taught him well, especially Latin and Greek. Later Charles worked for Mr Leeding as an assistant teacher."},{"sc":"college","n":"At fourteen, he went to an agricultural college in Maidstone for a year. He studied hard, and he was never afraid to ask hard questions, even of his teachers."},{"sc":"college","n":"At fifteen, he became an {usher}, an assistant teacher, at a school in {Newmarket}, a town famous for horse racing."},{"sc":"college","w":"charlieteen","e":"smile","t":"I'm only fifteen, but I'm teaching boys almost my own age. I'd better know my stuff!"},{"sc":"college","n":"He taught by day and read books by night. He read everything he could find: history, science, sermons, poetry and, of course, the Bible."},{"sc":"college","n":"But all the knowledge in the world couldn't give him what he really needed. That would come from somewhere he never expected."}],"quote":{"text":"Take fast hold of instruction; let her not go: keep her; for she is thy life.","who":"Proverbs 4:13","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Love learning","text":"Charles was a hungry learner all his life, reading everything he could find. Studying hard is a way of honouring God with your mind. But remember: knowledge is good, but it can't save you. Only Jesus can."},"think":"Charles taught boys nearly his own age at fifteen. What could you teach someone younger than you?","quiz":[{"q":"What subjects was Charles especially good at?","o":["English and maths","Football and dancing","Cooking and sewing","Nothing"],"why":"He often came top."},{"q":"Who taught Charles Latin and Greek in Colchester?","o":["Mr Leeding","Mr Knill","Old Roads","His grandfather"],"why":"Later Charles worked for him."},{"q":"What job did Charles have at fifteen?","o":["Assistant teacher (usher)","Jockey","Baker","Sailor"],"why":"At Newmarket."},{"q":"What was Newmarket famous for?","o":["Horse racing","Fishing","Mountains","Castles"],"why":"A busy town."},{"q":"What couldn't all Charles's knowledge give him?","o":["Peace with God","Good marks","A job","Books"],"why":"Only Jesus could."}]},"cs2s3":{"story":[{"sc":"kitchen","n":"At the school in Newmarket, there was a cook named Mary King. She was an older woman, and she went to the local chapel."},{"sc":"kitchen","n":"Mary King had never been to college. But she knew her Bible deeply, and she loved to talk about God's grace."},{"sc":"kitchen","w":"maryking","e":"smile","t":"Young Master Spurgeon! Sit down. Have some soup. Now, what did you think of the sermon on Sunday?"},{"sc":"kitchen","w":"charlieteen","e":"worried","t":"It was all about trying harder, Mrs King. But I've tried and tried, and I still feel so far from God."},{"sc":"kitchen","w":"maryking","e":"smile","t":"Ah, but it isn't about your trying, lad. It's about God's grace. He saves those who can't save themselves."},{"sc":"kitchen","n":"They talked for hours in that kitchen: about grace, about how God chooses and keeps his people, about the cross. She taught him a lot of {theology}, the study of God."},{"sc":"kitchen","n":"Years later, Spurgeon said that he learned more about God's truth from that cook than from many highly educated doctors of divinity."},{"sc":"kitchen","n":"Isn't that wonderful? One of history's greatest preachers was taught by a cook in a school kitchen. God uses ordinary people to teach his truth."}],"quote":{"text":"I do believe that I learned more from her than I should have learned from any six doctors of divinity.","who":"C. H. Spurgeon, about Mary King","tag":"Spurgeon's words"},"lesson":{"title":"Learn from anyone","text":"The future Prince of Preachers learned deep truths from a school cook. Don't look down on anyone because of their job or education. Some of the wisest teachers you'll ever meet will be ordinary people who know God well."},"think":"Who is an 'ordinary' person in your life (a cleaner, a cook, a driver, a grandparent) who knows God deeply? What could you learn from them?","quiz":[{"q":"Who was Mary King?","o":["The cook at the Newmarket school","Charles's aunt","A teacher","A queen"],"why":"She knew her Bible deeply."},{"q":"What did Mary King teach Charles about?","o":["God's grace","Cooking","Horse racing","Latin"],"why":"God saves those who can't save themselves."},{"q":"Where did they talk?","o":["In the school kitchen","In a pub","At church only","By letter"],"why":"For hours."},{"q":"What did Spurgeon later say about her?","o":["He learned more from her than from six doctors of divinity","She was a bad cook","She was boring","He forgot her"],"why":"A cook taught a great preacher."},{"q":"In Acts 18:26, who explained God's way more perfectly to Apollos?","o":["Aquila and Priscilla","Peter and John","Paul and Silas","Mary and Martha"],"why":"Ordinary believers teaching a great preacher."}]},"cs2s4":{"story":[{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"Back home in Colchester, Charles was desperate. He decided he would go to every church and chapel in the town until he found out how he could be saved."},{"sc":"church","n":"He went to one chapel. The preacher spoke about being good and obeying God's law. That was true. But it didn't tell Charles how a sinner could be forgiven."},{"sc":"church","w":"preacher1","e":"stern","t":"You must be holy, you must keep God's commands, you must do your duty..."},{"sc":"church","w":"charlieteen","e":"worried","t":"I know I should! But I can't! Is there no one who can tell me how to be saved?"},{"sc":"church","n":"He went to another, and another. Some preachers spoke beautifully. Some were very clever. But none of them said the simple thing his heart needed to hear."},{"sc":"church","n":"Spurgeon later wrote that he would have given anything just to hear someone tell him plainly how he could be forgiven."},{"sc":"night","n":"Charles kept searching. He didn't give up, even when it felt hopeless. And Jesus has promised: 'Seek, and ye shall find.'"},{"sc":"night","n":"He was closer than he knew."}],"quote":{"text":"Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you.","who":"Matthew 7:7","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Keep seeking","text":"Charles searched chapel after chapel without finding peace, but he didn't give up. If you're searching for God, keep asking, seeking and knocking. Jesus promised that those who seek will find. And if you know the way, tell it plainly to others who are searching."},"think":"Charles wished someone would just tell him plainly how to be saved. How would you explain it simply to a friend?","quiz":[{"q":"What did Charles decide to do in Colchester?","o":["Go to every chapel until he found out how to be saved","Leave school","Move to London","Stop going to church"],"why":"He was desperate."},{"q":"What did the preachers mostly talk about?","o":["Being good and doing your duty","How a sinner can be forgiven","Football","Food"],"why":"True, but not what he needed."},{"q":"What did Charles want to hear?","o":["Plainly, how a sinner could be forgiven","A funny story","A long speech","Nothing"],"why":"He would have given anything."},{"q":"Did Charles give up?","o":["No, he kept searching","Yes","He stopped going to church","He moved away"],"why":"Seek, and ye shall find."},{"q":"What does Jesus promise in Matthew 7:7?","o":["Seek, and ye shall find","Seek, and you'll be disappointed","Don't bother seeking","Only the clever will find"],"why":"Ask, seek, knock."}]},"cs2s5":{"story":[{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"Sunday 6 January 1850. Charles was fifteen. He woke to find Colchester buried in snow, and more was falling."},{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"He set off to walk to the chapel he had planned to visit that morning. But the snowstorm grew so heavy that he couldn't go any further."},{"sc":"snowstreet","w":"charlieteen","e":"worried","t":"I can't see the road. I'll never get there in this. I'll have to stop somewhere."},{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"He turned down a side street called Artillery Street. There stood a small, plain chapel. It belonged to the {Primitive Methodist} people."},{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"Charles had heard about the Primitive Methodists. People said they sang so loudly it gave you a headache! But he was cold, and the door was open, so in he went."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Inside, there were only about twelve or fifteen people. The snow had kept most people at home."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Charles sat down under the gallery, near the back. They waited for the preacher."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"And waited. The minister never arrived. He was snowed in somewhere, probably miles away."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Charles must have thought the morning was wasted. But God had planned this snowstorm. And something was about to happen that would change his life, and millions of others."}],"quote":{"text":"For as the rain cometh down, and the snow from heaven, and returneth not thither... so shall my word be that goeth forth out of my mouth.","who":"Isaiah 55:10-11","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"God uses the snowstorm","text":"A snowstorm ruined Charles's plans, and an absent preacher seemed like a disaster. But God used both to bring him exactly where he needed to be. When your plans are 'snowed in', God may be leading you somewhere better."},"think":"Has something ever gone 'wrong' in your plans that turned out to be God's way of leading you somewhere good?","quiz":[{"q":"On what date was the famous snowstorm?","o":["Sunday 6 January 1850","Christmas Day 1834","19 June 1834","1 May 1850"],"why":"Charles was fifteen."},{"q":"Why did Charles stop on Artillery Street?","o":["The snowstorm was too heavy to go further","He was lost","He was hungry","A friend called him"],"why":"He couldn't see the road."},{"q":"What kind of chapel was it?","o":["Primitive Methodist","Catholic","Baptist","Anglican cathedral"],"why":"A small, plain chapel."},{"q":"How many people were there?","o":["About twelve or fifteen","About a thousand","Nobody","About two hundred"],"why":"The snow kept others at home."},{"q":"What happened to the minister?","o":["He never arrived, snowed in somewhere","He arrived late","He preached for hours","He sang a solo"],"why":"God had a plan."}]}}},"cs3":{"challenge":[{"q":"Where did Charles sit in the chapel?","o":["Under the gallery","In the front row","In the pulpit","Outside"],"why":"Near the back."},{"q":"What job did Spurgeon think the thin preacher had?","o":["Shoemaker or tailor","Doctor","Lawyer","Bishop"],"why":"Not a trained preacher."},{"q":"Complete the text: \"Look unto me, and be ye ___.\"","o":["saved","rich","happy","strong"],"why":"Isaiah 45:22."},{"q":"What did the preacher say you don't need in order to look?","o":["To go to college","Eyes","Faith","Jesus"],"why":"Anyone can look."},{"q":"What did the preacher notice about Charles?","o":["He looked very miserable","He was asleep","He was laughing","He was late"],"why":"And he spoke to him directly."},{"q":"How did Charles describe his conversion?","o":["The darkness rolled away and he saw the sun","A loud bang","A long sleep","A dream"],"why":"A moment of light."},{"q":"Where was Charles baptised?","o":["The River Lark at Isleham","The River Thames","The River Niger","The Jordan"],"why":"On 3 May 1850."},{"q":"Whose birthday was 3 May?","o":["His mother's","His father's","His grandfather's","The Queen's"],"why":"He chose it on purpose."},{"q":"What did Charles give out in Newmarket?","o":["Handwritten tracts","Sweets","Money","Newspapers"],"why":"Sharing the good news."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Now is the day of ___.\"","o":["salvation","rest","judgment","fun"],"why":"2 Corinthians 6:2."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! How many times did the preacher shout \"Look!\" at the end?","o":["Three","One","Ten","Never"],"why":"Look! Look! Look!"},"choices":[{"title":"Trying too hard","q":"You feel you must be really, really good before God will accept you, so you keep trying harder and feeling worse.","o":[{"t":"Stop trying to earn it. Look to Jesus, who has already done everything needed.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! 'You have nothing to do but to look and live.'"},{"t":"Try even harder.","f":"That's what kept Charles miserable for five years."},{"t":"Give up on God.","f":"God isn't asking you to be perfect first."}]},{"title":"The simple message","q":"Your friend asks, \"What do Christians actually believe? Keep it short!\"","o":[{"t":"Say simply: \"We believe Jesus died and rose again for us, and anyone who looks to him and trusts him is saved.\"","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Simple is powerful, just like the thin preacher's sermon."},{"t":"Give a confusing two-hour lecture.","f":"Sometimes less is more."},{"t":"Say you don't know.","f":"You can always share the simple heart of it."}]},{"title":"Going public","q":"Your youth group is holding a baptism service, and you've trusted Jesus. But you're nervous about everyone watching.","o":[{"t":"Talk to your pastor and parents about being baptised, even if you're nervous.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Charles walked eight miles to be baptised publicly."},{"t":"Keep your faith completely secret.","f":"Faith starts in the heart but shows on the outside."},{"t":"Do it just because your friends are.","f":"Make it your own decision, because you trust Jesus."}]},{"title":"Can't keep quiet","q":"You've just experienced God answering a big prayer. You're excited, but worried people will think you're weird if you mention it.","o":[{"t":"Share it naturally with a friend: \"Guess what God did?\"","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Come and hear what he hath done for my soul."},{"t":"Never mention it to anyone.","f":"Good news is meant to be shared."},{"t":"Brag about how spiritual you are.","f":"Share about God, not about yourself."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 3 of Spurgeon's story in real life."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"👀 Look to Jesus, not to yourself. That's all it takes to be saved."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"⏰ Today is the day. You don't need to fix yourself first."},{"sc":"dawn","n":"☀️ Let the burden roll away. Jesus has done it all."},{"sc":"river","n":"💧 Don't keep your faith hidden. Let it show."},{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"📜 Share the good news simply, starting right where you are."}],"stops":{"cs3s1":{"story":[{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"The minister wasn't coming. The little congregation looked at each other. Somebody had to do something."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"At last, a thin-looking man stood up and went into the pulpit. Spurgeon thought he was a shoemaker, or a tailor, or something like that. He was certainly not a trained preacher."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"He opened his Bible and read his text, from {Isaiah 45:22}: 'Look unto me, and be ye saved, all the ends of the earth.'"},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"He couldn't even say all the words properly. But he had one thing in his favour: he had a simple text, and he stuck to it."},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"smile","t":"My dear friends, this is a very simple text indeed. It says, 'Look.'"},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"big","t":"Now, looking doesn't take a lot of effort. It isn't lifting your foot or your finger. It's just, 'Look.'"},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"big","t":"A man doesn't need to go to college to learn to look. You may be the biggest fool, and yet you can look. Anyone can look. Even a child can look."},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"smile","t":"But the text says, 'Look unto ME.' Many of you are looking to yourselves. That's no use. You'll never find comfort in yourselves."},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"big","t":"Jesus Christ says, 'Look unto me. I am sweating great drops of blood. Look unto me. I am hanging on the cross. Look unto me. I am dead and buried. Look unto me. I rise again! Look unto me. I am sitting at the Father's right hand. O poor sinner, look unto me!'"},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Charles listened. For five years, he had been trying hard to save himself. And this simple man was saying: you don't have to do anything. Just look to Jesus."}],"quote":{"text":"My dear friends, this is a very simple text indeed. It says, 'Look.'","who":"The thin preacher, as Spurgeon remembered","tag":"Spurgeon's words"},"lesson":{"title":"Simple is powerful","text":"The preacher wasn't educated or eloquent. But he stuck to one simple truth: look to Jesus. God doesn't need clever words to change a heart. The simplest message about Jesus is the most powerful one."},"think":"Why do you think God used an uneducated man, not a famous preacher, to reach Charles?","quiz":[{"q":"Who preached when the minister didn't come?","o":["A thin man, perhaps a shoemaker or tailor","Charles","A bishop","Nobody"],"why":"Not a trained preacher."},{"q":"What was his text?","o":["Isaiah 45:22, 'Look unto me, and be ye saved'","John 3:16","Genesis 1:1","Psalm 23"],"why":"He stuck to it."},{"q":"What did he say about looking?","o":["It doesn't take effort; anyone can look","It's very hard","Only clever people can look","You need college to look"],"why":"Even a child can look."},{"q":"Who did he say to look to?","o":["Jesus, not yourselves","Yourselves","The preacher","The church"],"why":"'Look unto ME.'"},{"q":"What had Charles been trying to do for five years?","o":["Save himself by effort","Become rich","Leave school","Learn to sing"],"why":"Now he heard: just look."}]},"cs3s2":{"story":[{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"After about ten minutes, the thin man ran out of things to say. He had preached everything he knew."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Then he looked across the little chapel, at the young stranger sitting under the gallery. With so few people there, he could easily see that Charles was new."},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"stern","t":"Young man, you look very miserable."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Charles was startled. He certainly was miserable! But he wasn't used to preachers talking to him personally from the pulpit."},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"stern","t":"And you always will be miserable, miserable in life and miserable in death, if you don't obey my text."},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"big","t":"But if you obey now, this moment, you will be saved!"},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Then he lifted up his hands and shouted, as only a Primitive Methodist could:"},{"sc":"snowchapel","w":"thinman","e":"big","t":"Young man, look to Jesus Christ! Look! Look! Look! You have nothing to do but to look and live!"},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"The words went straight into Charles's heart, like an arrow."}],"quote":{"text":"Young man, look to Jesus Christ. Look! Look! Look! You have nothin' to do but to look and live.","who":"The thin preacher, as Spurgeon remembered","tag":"Spurgeon's words"},"lesson":{"title":"Today is the day","text":"The preacher didn't say 'one day' or 'when you're older'. He said: 'if you obey now, this moment'. God doesn't ask you to fix yourself first. You can look to Jesus right now, exactly as you are."},"think":"Why do you think the preacher said 'now, this moment'? Is there anything stopping you from looking to Jesus today?","quiz":[{"q":"How long did the thin man preach before running out of things to say?","o":["About ten minutes","Two hours","Thirty seconds","All day"],"why":"He preached all he knew."},{"q":"What did he say to Charles?","o":["Young man, you look very miserable","Young man, go home","Young man, sit still","Young man, sing louder"],"why":"Charles certainly was."},{"q":"When did he say Charles should obey?","o":["Now, this moment","When he was older","Next week","After college"],"why":"Today is the day."},{"q":"What did he shout three times?","o":["Look!","Run!","Pray!","Sing!"],"why":"Look and live!"},{"q":"What does 2 Corinthians 6:2 say?","o":["Now is the day of salvation","Salvation is for later","Wait until you're ready","It's too late"],"why":"Now is the accepted time."}]},"cs3s3":{"story":[{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Charles did what the man said. Right there, under the gallery, on that snowy Sunday morning, he looked to Jesus."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Not to his own efforts. Not to his prayers or his good behaviour. Just to Jesus, who died for him and rose again."},{"sc":"dawn","n":"And in that moment, everything changed. Spurgeon later described it like this: the cloud was gone, the darkness had rolled away, and he saw the sun."},{"sc":"dawn","w":"charlieteen","e":"big","t":"I can see it! It's not about me trying. It's about Jesus. He has done it all. I just have to look to him!"},{"sc":"dawn","n":"He said he looked and looked until he could almost have looked his eyes away. The burden he had carried for five years was gone, like Christian's burden rolling away at the cross."},{"sc":"dawn","n":"He wanted to sing with the Primitive Methodists, as loud as they did. He wanted to shout about the precious blood of Jesus."},{"sc":"home","n":"When he got home, his mother looked at him and saw that something had changed. His face was different. He was happy."},{"sc":"home","w":"elizaspurgeon","e":"shock","t":"Charles, something wonderful has happened to you!"},{"sc":"home","n":"For the rest of his life, Spurgeon told people about that snowy morning. And he preached the same simple message millions of times: look to Jesus and live."}],"quote":{"text":"I looked until I could almost have looked my eyes away.","who":"C. H. Spurgeon, remembering 6 January 1850","tag":"Spurgeon's words"},"lesson":{"title":"Look and live","text":"After five years of trying, Charles found peace in one moment, by simply looking to Jesus. Salvation isn't earned by effort. It's received by faith. Whoever looks to Jesus is saved, whatever they've done."},"think":"Charles compared it to the sun breaking through clouds. How would you describe what it means to trust in Jesus?","quiz":[{"q":"What did Charles do in the chapel?","o":["Looked to Jesus","Left early","Fell asleep","Argued"],"why":"Not to his own efforts."},{"q":"How did Spurgeon describe the moment?","o":["The darkness rolled away and he saw the sun","Nothing happened","It got darker","He felt sick"],"why":"Everything changed."},{"q":"How long did he say he looked?","o":["Until he could almost have looked his eyes away","Two seconds","He didn't look","Until lunch"],"why":"He couldn't stop!"},{"q":"Who noticed something had changed when he got home?","o":["His mother","His dog","His teacher","Nobody"],"why":"His face was different."},{"q":"In Numbers 21:8, what happened to people who looked at the bronze serpent?","o":["They lived","They died","They ran","They slept"],"why":"Jesus compared himself to it (John 3:14)."}]},"cs3s4":{"story":[{"sc":"college","n":"Back at Newmarket, Charles studied what the Bible taught about baptism. He became convinced that people should be baptised when they believe for themselves."},{"sc":"college","n":"His family weren't {Baptist}s. So Charles wrote to his parents and asked their permission to be baptised. After a while, they agreed."},{"sc":"road","n":"On 3 May 1850, Charles got up early and walked about eight miles to a place called Isleham, on the {River Lark}."},{"sc":"river","n":"There, a minister named Mr Cantlow baptised him in the river. Charles went down under the cold water and came up again, as a sign that his old life had died and he was alive in Christ."},{"sc":"river","w":"cantlow","e":"smile","t":"Charles Spurgeon, I baptise you in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit."},{"sc":"river","n":"Charles had chosen that day on purpose. 3 May was his mother's birthday."},{"sc":"home","n":"His mother had once said she prayed that Charles would become a Christian, but she never prayed he would become a Baptist!"},{"sc":"home","w":"charlieteen","e":"big","t":"Ah, Mother! The Lord has answered your prayer with his usual generosity, and given you far more than you asked!"},{"sc":"home","n":"His mother laughed. She had prayed for her son to be saved, and God had answered, in his own way."}],"quote":{"text":"Therefore we are buried with him by baptism into death: that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life.","who":"Romans 6:4","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Going public","text":"Charles didn't keep his faith secret. He was baptised publicly, as a sign that he belonged to Jesus. Following Jesus starts in the heart, but it doesn't stay hidden. It shows on the outside."},"think":"Why do you think it mattered to Charles to be baptised publicly, not just believe privately?","quiz":[{"q":"When was Charles baptised?","o":["3 May 1850","6 January 1850","19 June 1834","1 January 1851"],"why":"In the River Lark."},{"q":"How far did he walk to get there?","o":["About eight miles","Two hundred miles","Next door","He rode a horse"],"why":"He got up early."},{"q":"What did Charles ask his parents before being baptised?","o":["Their permission","For money","For a horse","Nothing"],"why":"He honoured them."},{"q":"Why did he choose 3 May?","o":["It was his mother's birthday","It was Christmas","It was his birthday","By accident"],"why":"A special gift."},{"q":"What did Charles joke to his mother?","o":["God answered her prayer with his usual generosity","She should have prayed harder","He was only joking","Nothing"],"why":"More than she asked!"}]},"cs3s5":{"story":[{"sc":"college","n":"Once Charles had found Jesus, he couldn't keep quiet about it. He wanted everyone to know."},{"sc":"study","n":"He started writing out short messages about Jesus by hand, on little pieces of paper, and giving them away. These were called {tracts}."},{"sc":"snowstreet","n":"He walked around Newmarket on Saturday afternoons, visiting homes, handing out tracts and talking to people about Jesus."},{"sc":"snowstreet","w":"charlieteen","e":"smile","t":"Good afternoon! May I leave this with you? It's about the best news I ever heard."},{"sc":"church","n":"He also started teaching a Sunday School class. Soon the children loved his stories so much that adults started coming to listen too!"},{"sc":"church","n":"He was only fifteen and sixteen. He wasn't a preacher yet. He was just a young believer who couldn't stop sharing the good news."},{"sc":"church","w":"charlieteen","e":"big","t":"I can't be silent. If someone saved you from drowning, wouldn't you tell everyone?"},{"sc":"church","n":"Big things in God's kingdom often start like this: with small, faithful, everyday sharing."}],"quote":{"text":"Come and hear, all ye that fear God, and I will declare what he hath done for my soul.","who":"Psalm 66:16","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Share what you've found","text":"Charles didn't wait to be a famous preacher. As a teenager, he wrote tracts by hand, visited homes and taught Sunday school. You don't have to be grown up to tell others about Jesus. Start where you are."},"think":"What's one small way you could share about Jesus this week: a kind word, a verse, an invitation?","quiz":[{"q":"What are tracts?","o":["Short leaflets about the good news of Jesus","Train tracks","Football boots","Bags"],"why":"Charles wrote them by hand."},{"q":"What did Charles do on Saturday afternoons?","o":["Visited homes and gave out tracts","Went to the races","Played cards","Slept"],"why":"He couldn't keep quiet."},{"q":"What happened in his Sunday School class?","o":["Adults started coming to listen too","Nobody came","It closed","He quit"],"why":"The children loved his stories."},{"q":"How old was Charles at this time?","o":["Fifteen and sixteen","Thirty","Six","Twenty-five"],"why":"Just a young believer."},{"q":"What does Psalm 66:16 say?","o":["I will declare what he hath done for my soul","Keep quiet about God","Only preachers can share","Hide your faith"],"why":"Come and hear!"}]}}},"cs4":{"challenge":[{"q":"Why did Charles move to Cambridge?","o":["To work as an assistant teacher","To study at the university","To join the army","To be a pastor"],"why":"At Mr Leeding's school."},{"q":"What was Mr Vinter's trick?","o":["He told each young man the other would preach","He hid the Bible","He sent them to the wrong village","He locked the door"],"why":"Neither had preached before!"},{"q":"Where did Charles preach his first sermon?","o":["Teversham","London","Waterbeach","Colchester"],"why":"In a thatched cottage."},{"q":"When did Charles become pastor at Waterbeach?","o":["October 1851","January 1850","December 1853","June 1834"],"why":"At seventeen."},{"q":"How far was Waterbeach from Cambridge?","o":["About six miles","Sixty miles","Six hundred miles","Next door"],"why":"He walked or rode on Sundays."},{"q":"What changed in Waterbeach homes?","o":["Drunkards became sober and families prayed","Nothing","Everyone moved away","They built a pub"],"why":"Changed lives."},{"q":"Who did Charles miss meeting because of the maid's mistake?","o":["Dr Angus","Mr Vinter","Queen Victoria","Mr Knill"],"why":"They were in different rooms."},{"q":"What verse convinced Charles not to go to college?","o":["Jeremiah 45:5","John 3:16","Psalm 23","Genesis 1:1"],"why":"Seek them not."},{"q":"In what month and year did Charles go to preach in London?","o":["December 1853","January 1850","October 1851","June 1856"],"why":"With a small bag."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Let no man despise thy ___.\"","o":["youth","money","house","name"],"why":"1 Timothy 4:12."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did Charles tell the old woman when she asked his age after the service?","o":["That he was under sixty","That he was a hundred","That it was a secret","That he was forty"],"why":"'Yes,' she said, 'and under sixteen!'"},"choices":[{"title":"Unexpected opportunity","q":"Your youth leader suddenly asks you to share a short testimony at fellowship, right now.","o":[{"t":"Take a breath, say a quick prayer, and share simply what Jesus means to you.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Charles preached his first sermon by surprise, about how precious Jesus is."},{"t":"Refuse. You're not ready.","f":"If you know Jesus, you have something to share."},{"t":"Make something up to sound impressive.","f":"Simple and true beats impressive."}]},{"title":"Too young?","q":"You want to start a lunchtime Bible club at school, but some older students say you're too young to lead anything.","o":[{"t":"Talk to a teacher for support, and start small anyway.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Let no man despise thy youth. Charles pastored a church at seventeen."},{"t":"Give up. They're probably right.","f":"God uses young people."},{"t":"Argue with them angrily.","f":"Be an example, not an arguer."}]},{"title":"Chasing greatness","q":"You're offered a chance to join a famous club, but it means quitting the small group of younger kids you've been helping.","o":[{"t":"Pray about it honestly, and don't choose just because it looks impressive.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! 'Seekest thou great things for thyself? Seek them not.'"},{"t":"Take it immediately. Fame is everything.","f":"Charles learned to seek God's plan, not a big name."},{"t":"Refuse without thinking.","f":"Not every big opportunity is wrong. Pray and seek God's will."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"hut","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 4 of Spurgeon's story in real life."},{"sc":"hut","n":"🎤 Be ready to share what Jesus means to you, even without warning."},{"sc":"hall","n":"🌾 Let no one despise your youth. Be an example now."},{"sc":"fens","n":"🏘️ Real faith changes ordinary homes and habits."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"🚪 Don't chase greatness for yourself. Seek God's plan."},{"sc":"night","n":"✉️ Say, 'Speak, Lord, I'm listening', and be ready for surprises."}],"stops":{"cs4s1":{"story":[{"sc":"college","n":"In 1850, Charles moved to {Cambridge} to work as an assistant teacher at his old teacher Mr Leeding's new school. He joined a Baptist church there and taught Sunday school."},{"sc":"college","n":"In Cambridge there was a group of {lay preacher}s who went out to preach in the villages on Sunday evenings. Their leader was a kind old gentleman named Mr Vinter."},{"sc":"college","w":"vinter","e":"smile","t":"Spurgeon, would you go over to {Teversham} next Sunday evening? A young man is going to preach there, and he's not used to it. He'd be glad of some company."},{"sc":"fens","n":"Charles agreed. As they walked along the country lane, Charles said to his companion that he hoped God would bless his preaching that night."},{"sc":"fens","w":"companion","e":"shock","t":"My preaching? I've never preached in my life! I was told YOU were going to preach!"},{"sc":"fens","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"shock","t":"Me? I've never preached either!"},{"sc":"fens","w":"companion","e":"worried","t":"Well, if you don't preach, there won't be a sermon at all."},{"sc":"fens","n":"Mr Vinter had tricked them both! Charles walked on, praying hard. He thought: surely I can tell these village people about the love of Jesus."},{"sc":"hut","n":"In a little thatched cottage, a few farm workers and their wives had gathered. Charles stood up. He was just sixteen. He chose his text: 'Unto you therefore which believe he is precious.'"},{"sc":"hut","n":"He spoke about how precious Jesus is. And to his own surprise, the words flowed. He finished, and before he could sit down, an old woman called out."},{"sc":"hut","w":"oldwoman","e":"shock","t":"Bless your dear heart, how old are you?"},{"sc":"hut","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"You must wait till the service is over before asking that. Let's sing a hymn!"},{"sc":"hut","n":"Afterwards, they all laughed together. Charles told them he was under sixty. 'Yes,' said the old woman, 'and under sixteen!' Spurgeon's preaching life had begun."}],"quote":{"text":"Unto you therefore which believe he is precious.","who":"1 Peter 2:7","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Ready when called","text":"Charles didn't plan to preach that night, but because he knew Jesus and loved him, he had something to say. Be ready! You never know when you'll be asked to share about your faith."},"think":"If someone asked you, without warning, to share what Jesus means to you, what would you say?","quiz":[{"q":"Who sent Charles to Teversham?","o":["Mr Vinter","Mr Knill","His mother","Mr Leeding"],"why":"A kind old gentleman with a plan."},{"q":"What surprise did Charles get on the way?","o":["His companion had never preached either","It rained","The village was empty","He got lost"],"why":"Mr Vinter had tricked them both!"},{"q":"How old was Charles at his first sermon?","o":["Sixteen","Twelve","Twenty-one","Thirty"],"why":"Just a teenager."},{"q":"What was his text?","o":["Unto you therefore which believe he is precious","Look unto me","In the beginning","Jesus wept"],"why":"1 Peter 2:7."},{"q":"What did the old woman ask?","o":["How old are you?","What's your name?","Where are you from?","Can you sing?"],"why":"He made her wait till after the service!"}]},"cs4s2":{"story":[{"sc":"fens","n":"After that first sermon, Charles was asked to preach in village after village. He walked miles on Sunday evenings across the flat {fens}, in all weathers."},{"sc":"fens","n":"In October 1851, a little Baptist chapel in the village of {Waterbeach} asked him to become their pastor. He was just seventeen years old!"},{"sc":"fens","w":"waterbeachman","e":"shock","t":"Our new pastor's a boy! He still looks like a schoolboy!"},{"sc":"fens","n":"Waterbeach had a bad reputation. It was known for drunkenness, fighting and rough living. The chapel was a small, thatched building with only about forty people."},{"sc":"fens","n":"Charles still taught at the school in Cambridge during the week. On Sundays he walked or rode the six miles to Waterbeach to preach."},{"sc":"hall","n":"People came to hear 'the boy preacher'. At first, out of curiosity. Then because something was happening. The little chapel filled up, then overflowed."},{"sc":"hall","n":"Soon around four hundred people were crowding in, in a village that had once hardly cared about God."},{"sc":"hall","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"big","t":"I'm only a boy. But God's word isn't a boy's word. It is mighty, whoever speaks it."}],"quote":{"text":"Let no man despise thy youth; but be thou an example of the believers, in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity.","who":"1 Timothy 4:12","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Young and used by God","text":"Charles became a pastor at seventeen, and God used him powerfully. Young people don't have to wait to be grown up to serve God. Be an example now: in your words, actions, love, faith and purity."},"think":"Which of the six areas in 1 Timothy 4:12 (word, conversation, charity, spirit, faith, purity) could you be an example in this week?","quiz":[{"q":"How old was Charles when he became pastor at Waterbeach?","o":["Seventeen","Twenty-five","Ten","Forty"],"why":"In October 1851."},{"q":"What was Waterbeach known for?","o":["Drunkenness and rough living","Being a holy village","Its castle","Its university"],"why":"A bad reputation."},{"q":"How many people were in the chapel at first?","o":["About forty","About four thousand","None","Ten thousand"],"why":"A small, thatched building."},{"q":"How many came later?","o":["Around four hundred","Five","Forty","None"],"why":"It overflowed."},{"q":"What does 1 Timothy 4:12 tell young people?","o":["Be an example of the believers","Wait until you're old","Stay quiet","Let others lead"],"why":"Let no man despise thy youth."}]},"cs4s3":{"story":[{"sc":"fens","n":"Something even more amazing than crowds was happening in Waterbeach. People's lives were changing."},{"sc":"inn","n":"Men who had spent every evening drunk at the pub began to come home sober. Families who had fought and shouted began to pray together."},{"sc":"hut","w":"waterbeachman","e":"smile","t":"I used to spend all my wages on drink. Now my children have shoes, and we read the Bible together at night."},{"sc":"fens","n":"Spurgeon later remembered walking down the village street and seeing homes that had once been full of trouble, now full of peace and prayer."},{"sc":"fens","n":"The young pastor visited people in their homes, sat with the sick, and comforted families when someone died."},{"sc":"fens","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"This is the best proof that the gospel is true: it changes people."},{"sc":"fens","n":"Charles loved his people at Waterbeach, and they loved him. Many years later, when he was world-famous, he still spoke warmly of that little village chapel in the fens."}],"quote":{"text":"To give unto them beauty for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning, the garment of praise for the spirit of heaviness.","who":"Isaiah 61:3","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Changed lives prove it","text":"The best proof of the gospel in Waterbeach wasn't big crowds. It was changed homes: sober fathers, praying families. When Jesus changes people, it shows in ordinary life."},"think":"What would it look like if Jesus changed your street, school or town the way he changed Waterbeach?","quiz":[{"q":"What was even more amazing than the crowds?","o":["People's lives were changing","The building","The music","The money"],"why":"Real change."},{"q":"What happened to some men who used to drink?","o":["They came home sober","They drank more","They left the village","Nothing"],"why":"Families began to pray."},{"q":"What did the young pastor do besides preaching?","o":["Visited homes, sat with the sick, comforted families","Nothing","Ran a shop","Played cricket"],"why":"He loved his people."},{"q":"What did Charles say was the best proof of the gospel?","o":["It changes people","Big buildings","Famous preachers","Money"],"why":"Changed lives."},{"q":"What does Isaiah 61:3 say God gives?","o":["Beauty for ashes","Ashes for beauty","Sadness for joy","Nothing"],"why":"And joy for mourning."}]},"cs4s4":{"story":[{"sc":"college","n":"Charles had never been to university or college to train as a minister. Some people thought he should go."},{"sc":"college","n":"In 1852, it was arranged for him to meet Dr Angus, the head of a Baptist college in London, at a house in Cambridge. If the meeting went well, Charles might go to study there."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"Charles arrived at the house at the right time and rang the bell. A maid opened the door."},{"sc":"parlour","w":"maid","e":"smile","t":"Please wait in here, sir."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"She showed him into a sitting room. Charles waited. And waited. Two hours went by. Nobody came."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"At last he rang the bell and asked what was happening. The maid had made a mistake. Dr Angus had been waiting in a different room in the same house. And now he had gone back to London on the train!"},{"sc":"fens","n":"Charles was disappointed. That afternoon, he went for a walk on {Midsummer Common} to think and pray."},{"sc":"fens","n":"As he walked, a Bible verse came into his mind with great force, almost as if he heard a voice: 'Seekest thou great things for thyself? Seek them not!'"},{"sc":"fens","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"Lord, I won't chase a big name or a grand career for myself. I'll stay with my people at Waterbeach, and go wherever you lead."},{"sc":"fens","n":"He decided not to go to college. Looking back, he was sure God had arranged that mix-up in the house. God had another way of training him: in the fens, among ordinary people."}],"quote":{"text":"And seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not.","who":"Jeremiah 45:5","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Don't chase greatness","text":"A maid's mistake changed Charles's whole path, and a verse taught him not to seek great things for himself. Work hard and learn all you can, but don't make your goal 'being great'. Make it serving God faithfully. He'll decide how big your life should be."},"think":"What's the difference between wanting to be great and wanting to serve God greatly?","quiz":[{"q":"Who was Charles supposed to meet?","o":["Dr Angus, head of a Baptist college","Queen Victoria","Mr Vinter","His mother"],"why":"To talk about college."},{"q":"What went wrong?","o":["The maid put them in different rooms","Charles was late","It snowed","Dr Angus was ill"],"why":"Two hours in different rooms!"},{"q":"Where did Charles walk afterwards?","o":["Midsummer Common","The River Lark","London Bridge","The Crystal Palace"],"why":"To think and pray."},{"q":"What verse came into his mind?","o":["Seekest thou great things for thyself? Seek them not","Look unto me","Go ye into all the world","Be still"],"why":"Jeremiah 45:5."},{"q":"What did Charles decide?","o":["Not to go to college, and stay with his people","To move to London at once","To stop preaching","To become a teacher only"],"why":"God had another way to train him."}]},"cs4s5":{"story":[{"sc":"fens","n":"By the end of 1853, Charles was nineteen. He had been pastor at Waterbeach for two years. Word about the boy preacher was spreading."},{"sc":"fens","n":"One Sunday morning in November, a letter arrived. It was from a deacon of New Park Street Chapel in London, one of the most famous Baptist churches in the country."},{"sc":"fens","n":"The letter asked Charles to come and preach at New Park Street."},{"sc":"fens","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"shock","t":"This must be a mistake! They can't mean me. There must be another Spurgeon!"},{"sc":"fens","n":"He handed the letter to one of the deacons at Waterbeach. The old deacon read it and shook his head."},{"sc":"fens","w":"waterbeachman","e":"worried","t":"No, it's no mistake, my boy. I've been afraid of this. They'll take you from us. A village like ours can't keep a preacher like you for long."},{"sc":"fens","n":"Charles wrote back to say that he was only nineteen, and that perhaps they had made an error. But they wrote again: they knew exactly how old he was. Would he please come?"},{"sc":"night","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"worried","t":"Lord, I'm only a country boy. But if you're calling me, I'll go. Speak, Lord. Your servant is listening."},{"sc":"londonfog","n":"So in December 1853, Charles Spurgeon packed a small bag and took the train to London. He had no idea what was waiting for him there."}],"quote":{"text":"Speak; for thy servant heareth.","who":"1 Samuel 3:10","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Listening for God's call","text":"Charles was sure the letter was a mistake. But God's call often surprises us. Like young Samuel, Charles said, 'Speak, Lord, I'm listening.' Stay humble, and be ready when God opens a door you never expected."},"think":"Why do you think Charles assumed the letter was a mistake? What does that tell you about him?","quiz":[{"q":"How old was Charles when the letter came?","o":["Nineteen","Sixteen","Thirty","Twelve"],"why":"At the end of 1853."},{"q":"Which chapel invited him?","o":["New Park Street Chapel, London","Waterbeach","Teversham","St Paul's"],"why":"A famous Baptist church."},{"q":"What did Charles think about the letter?","o":["It must be a mistake","It was exactly right","It was a joke from friends","Nothing"],"why":"There must be another Spurgeon!"},{"q":"What did the old Waterbeach deacon say?","o":["They'll take you from us","Tear it up","Don't go","It's a trick"],"why":"He'd been afraid of it."},{"q":"What did Samuel say to God?","o":["Speak; for thy servant heareth","Go away","Not now","Ask someone else"],"why":"1 Samuel 3:10."}]}}},"cs5":{"challenge":[{"q":"Where did Charles stay on his first night in London?","o":["A boarding house in Bloomsbury","A palace","A church","A hotel"],"why":"And was laughed at."},{"q":"How many people came on his first Sunday morning at New Park Street?","o":["About eighty","About eight thousand","About eight","About eight hundred"],"why":"In a chapel for twelve hundred."},{"q":"What did Spurgeon preach about on his first morning?","o":["God, the giver of every good gift","The snowstorm","Old Roads","Cholera"],"why":"James 1:17."},{"q":"When did Charles become pastor of New Park Street?","o":["April 1854","December 1853","January 1856","June 1850"],"why":"At nineteen."},{"q":"What was the big public hall they used?","o":["Exeter Hall","Royal Albert Hall","Buckingham Palace","Westminster Abbey"],"why":"While the chapel was enlarged."},{"q":"What disease struck London in 1854?","o":["Cholera","Plague","Smallpox","Malaria"],"why":"Thousands died."},{"q":"Who put Psalm 91 in his window?","o":["A shoemaker","A baker","A teacher","A soldier"],"why":"He never knew how much it helped."},{"q":"When did Charles marry Susannah?","o":["8 January 1856","10 June 1854","18 December 1853","3 May 1850"],"why":"In London."},{"q":"What were the twins' names?","o":["Charles and Thomas","John and James","Peter and Paul","Taiwo and Kehinde"],"why":"Both became preachers."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Thou, O LORD, art a ___ for me.\"","o":["shield","sword","house","crown"],"why":"Psalm 3:3."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did Susannah first laugh at about Charles?","o":["His enormous neck-cloth","His voice","His height","His shoes"],"why":"And then she married him!"},"choices":[{"title":"Laughed at for your accent","q":"You've moved to a new school in a big city. Some students laugh at your accent and call you a 'village kid'.","o":[{"t":"Stay kind and confident. God called you here, and he lifts up your head.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Charles was laughed at on his first night in London, and he went on to fill the city's biggest buildings."},{"t":"Change everything about yourself to fit in.","f":"Be yourself. God made you."},{"t":"Mock them back.","f":"That only makes things worse."}]},{"title":"Fear in a crisis","q":"There's a disease outbreak in your town and everyone is anxious. You can't stop worrying.","o":[{"t":"Take sensible precautions, read Psalm 91, and pray, trusting God as your refuge.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Faith took the place of fear for Charles, through a verse in a shop window."},{"t":"Panic and spread scary rumours.","f":"Fear spreads faster than truth."},{"t":"Ignore all safety advice.","f":"Trusting God doesn't mean being careless."}]},{"title":"First impressions","q":"A new girl at church dresses very differently and speaks very loudly. Your friends are already laughing about her.","o":[{"t":"Get to know her. Look past first impressions.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Susannah laughed at Charles's neck-cloth, then discovered his heart."},{"t":"Laugh along. First impressions are always right.","f":"They're often wrong."},{"t":"Avoid her.","f":"You might miss a friend for life."}]},{"title":"Mocked online","q":"Someone posts a mean comment about your Christian faith under your photo. Lots of people 'like' it.","o":[{"t":"Don't hit back. Remember Matthew 5:11-12, and keep living for Jesus.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Spurgeon kept preaching through years of mockery."},{"t":"Post something even meaner about them.","f":"Spurgeon didn't fight insults with insults."},{"t":"Delete everything about your faith.","f":"Don't let mockery silence you."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"parlour","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 5 of Spurgeon's story in real life."},{"sc":"night","n":"🧣 When you're mocked for being different, let God lift up your head."},{"sc":"hall","n":"⛪ Be faithful, not famous. Results are God's business."},{"sc":"londonfog","n":"🛡️ Let God's word replace fear. Share a verse; it might be perfectly timed."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"💐 Look deeper than first impressions, and pray about your future."},{"sc":"home","n":"📰 When mocked for Jesus, don't hit back. You are blessed."}],"stops":{"cs5s1":{"story":[{"sc":"londonfog","n":"On a cold Saturday evening in December 1853, Charles arrived in London. It was huge, foggy, noisy and frightening compared with the quiet fens."},{"sc":"londonfog","n":"He had been booked into a boarding house in Bloomsbury, where several young men from London were also staying."},{"sc":"parlour","n":"Charles walked in wearing his country clothes: a big black satin neck-cloth and a blue handkerchief with white spots. The young Londoners stared, and smirked."},{"sc":"parlour","w":"lodger","e":"smirk","t":"You're preaching at New Park Street? You? Ha! Do you know what great preachers London has? You'll never fill that chapel!"},{"sc":"parlour","n":"All evening, they told him stories about the brilliant, famous preachers of London, and made it clear they thought this country boy would be a disaster."},{"sc":"night","n":"That night, Charles lay on a narrow, hard bed in a tiny room. Through the window he could hear the noise of the city. He couldn't sleep."},{"sc":"night","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"worried","t":"Lord, what am I doing here? I'm just a country boy. I feel so alone."},{"sc":"night","n":"He felt miserable and out of place. But he prayed, and he remembered that it was God who had called him, not the young men in the boarding house."}],"quote":{"text":"But thou, O LORD, art a shield for me; my glory, and the lifter up of mine head.","who":"Psalm 3:3","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Mocked for being different","text":"On his first night in London, Charles was laughed at for his clothes and his country ways. It hurt. But their opinion didn't decide his future: God did. When people mock you for being different, let God lift up your head."},"think":"Have you ever been laughed at for your clothes, accent or background? How might Psalm 3:3 help you in that moment?","quiz":[{"q":"When did Charles arrive in London?","o":["December 1853","June 1834","January 1850","October 1856"],"why":"On a cold Saturday evening."},{"q":"What did the young men at the boarding house do?","o":["Laughed at him and his country clothes","Welcomed him warmly","Ignored him","Asked him to preach"],"why":"They smirked."},{"q":"What was Charles wearing?","o":["A black satin neck-cloth and a blue spotted handkerchief","A crown","A soldier's uniform","A robe"],"why":"Country clothes."},{"q":"What did they say about New Park Street?","o":["He'd never fill the chapel","It was tiny","It was closed","It was in Cambridge"],"why":"They were sure he'd fail."},{"q":"What does Psalm 3:3 call God?","o":["A shield, and the lifter up of my head","Far away","Angry","Asleep"],"why":"God lifted Charles's head."}]},"cs5s2":{"story":[{"sc":"londonfog","n":"On Sunday morning, 18 December 1853, Charles walked through the fog to {New Park Street Chapel} in {Southwark}."},{"sc":"hall","n":"The chapel was big enough for about twelve hundred people. But that morning, only about eighty or so were scattered around the pews. The church had shrunk for years."},{"sc":"hall","n":"Charles climbed into the high pulpit and preached about God, the giver of every good and perfect gift."},{"sc":"hall","n":"The people were amazed. This boy preached with such life, such warmth, such simple power! Between the morning and evening services, they hurried round London telling their friends."},{"sc":"hall","w":"olney","e":"big","t":"You must come back, Mr Spurgeon! Please, come back and preach again!"},{"sc":"hall","n":"That evening, the chapel was much fuller. Charles came back to preach again in the new year. Each time, more people came."},{"sc":"hall","n":"In April 1854, the church asked him to become their pastor. He was just nineteen years old."},{"sc":"hall","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"I'm very young. Pray for me. Pray that God's word will be preached here with power."},{"sc":"hall","n":"Within months, the chapel was packed, with crowds standing outside. They had to enlarge the building. While it was being rebuilt, they met in the huge {Exeter Hall}. And that was packed too."}],"quote":{"text":"Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and cometh down from the Father of lights.","who":"James 1:17 (Spurgeon's first text at New Park Street)","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Faithful, not famous","text":"Charles didn't go to London trying to be famous. He just preached God's word faithfully, and God filled the empty chapel. Your job is faithfulness. Results are God's business."},"think":"Why do you think the people rushed to tell their friends about the young preacher?","quiz":[{"q":"When did Charles first preach at New Park Street?","o":["18 December 1853","6 January 1850","19 June 1834","8 January 1856"],"why":"A foggy Sunday morning."},{"q":"How many could the chapel hold?","o":["About twelve hundred","About fifty","About ten thousand","About five"],"why":"But only about eighty came that morning."},{"q":"What did people do between the services?","o":["Hurried to tell their friends","Went home to sleep","Complained","Left the church"],"why":"The evening was much fuller."},{"q":"How old was Charles when he became pastor?","o":["Nineteen","Twenty-five","Sixteen","Thirty"],"why":"In April 1854."},{"q":"Where did they meet while the chapel was enlarged?","o":["Exeter Hall","The Crystal Palace","A tent","A school"],"why":"And that was packed too."}]},"cs5s3":{"story":[{"sc":"londonfog","n":"In the summer of 1854, a terrible disease called {cholera} struck London. Thousands of people died in a few weeks."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"As the young pastor, Charles was called to sick beds and funerals day and night. Many in his own church died."},{"sc":"sickroom","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"worried","t":"I'm exhausted. So many funerals. I feel ill myself. What if I catch it too?"},{"sc":"londonfog","n":"One day, walking sadly home from a funeral, he passed a shoemaker's shop. In the window was a sheet of paper with large writing on it."},{"sc":"londonfog","n":"It was a verse from Psalm 91: 'Because thou hast made the LORD, which is my refuge, even the most High, thy habitation; there shall no evil befall thee, neither shall any plague come nigh thy dwelling.'"},{"sc":"londonfog","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"The Lord is my refuge. I'm in his hands. I don't need to be afraid."},{"sc":"londonfog","n":"Spurgeon wrote later that the effect on his heart was immediate. Faith took the place of fear. He felt calm and strong again."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"He went back to visiting the sick and dying without fear, comforting them and praying with them, all through that terrible summer."}],"quote":{"text":"There shall no evil befall thee, neither shall any plague come nigh thy dwelling.","who":"Psalm 91:10, in the shoemaker's window","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"A verse at the right time","text":"A shoemaker put a Bible verse in his window, and it came at exactly the right moment for an exhausted young pastor. God's word can strengthen us in our hardest days. And a small act, like sharing a verse, can encourage someone more than you'll ever know."},"think":"The shoemaker never knew how much his verse helped. Where could you put or share a Bible verse to encourage others?","quiz":[{"q":"What disease struck London in 1854?","o":["Cholera","Malaria","Measles","Flu"],"why":"Thousands died."},{"q":"What was Charles doing day and night?","o":["Visiting the sick and taking funerals","Hiding at home","Travelling abroad","Writing books"],"why":"He was exhausted."},{"q":"Where did he see the Bible verse?","o":["In a shoemaker's window","In a newspaper","On a ship","In a dream"],"why":"Walking home from a funeral."},{"q":"Which Psalm was it from?","o":["Psalm 91","Psalm 23","Psalm 1","Psalm 150"],"why":"The Lord is my refuge."},{"q":"How did the verse change Charles?","o":["Faith took the place of fear","He became more afraid","He left London","Nothing changed"],"why":"He went back to the sick without fear."}]},"cs5s4":{"story":[{"sc":"hall","n":"Among the people at New Park Street was a young woman named Susannah Thompson."},{"sc":"hall","n":"At first, she wasn't impressed by the new preacher at all! She thought his country clothes and his big neck-cloth looked rather funny."},{"sc":"hall","w":"susie","e":"smirk","t":"He preaches well, I suppose. But that enormous neck-cloth! And that spotted handkerchief!"},{"sc":"hall","n":"But as she listened to him preach week after week, her heart was stirred. She had drifted from God, and through his preaching she came back to Jesus with fresh love."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"On 10 June 1854, a group from the church went to see the grand opening of the {Crystal Palace}. Charles sat down next to Susannah."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"He was reading a book of wise sayings. He pointed to a passage about choosing a good husband or wife, and quietly asked:"},{"sc":"crystalpalace","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"Do you pray for the one who will be your husband?"},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"Susannah blushed bright red! But she understood exactly what he meant. They walked and talked together for the rest of the day."},{"sc":"church","n":"They married on 8 January 1856. Later that year, God gave them twin baby boys, Charles and Thomas. Both grew up to become preachers too."},{"sc":"church","w":"susie","e":"big","t":"I married the most wonderful man in the world. And I laughed at his neck-cloth!"}],"quote":{"text":"Whoso findeth a wife findeth a good thing, and obtaineth favour of the LORD.","who":"Proverbs 18:22","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Look deeper","text":"Susannah first judged Charles by his clothes, then discovered his heart for God. And Charles asked her a beautiful question: do you pray for your future husband? Look deeper than appearances, and pray about your future, even now."},"think":"Charles asked Susannah if she prayed for her future husband. Why might it be wise to start praying about your future family now?","quiz":[{"q":"What did Susannah think of Charles at first?","o":["His clothes looked funny","He was perfect","He was too old","She never noticed him"],"why":"That enormous neck-cloth!"},{"q":"How did his preaching change her?","o":["She came back to Jesus with fresh love","She left the church","Nothing changed","She became a preacher"],"why":"Her heart was stirred."},{"q":"Where did Charles ask his famous question?","o":["At the opening of the Crystal Palace","In church","By letter","On a ship"],"why":"On 10 June 1854."},{"q":"What did he ask her?","o":["Do you pray for the one who will be your husband?","Can you cook?","Do you like my neck-cloth?","Where do you live?"],"why":"She blushed!"},{"q":"What did God give them later in 1856?","o":["Twin baby boys","A house","A horse","A castle"],"why":"Charles and Thomas."}]},"cs5s5":{"story":[{"sc":"newsroom","n":"As crowds poured in to hear the young preacher, the newspapers began to write about him. And much of what they wrote was cruel."},{"sc":"newsroom","w":"reporter","e":"smirk","t":"This boy preacher is a showman, a clown! He talks like a market trader! It won't last. Give it a year."},{"sc":"newsroom","n":"Cartoons made fun of him. Writers called him vulgar, conceited, a passing fad. Some other ministers criticised him too."},{"sc":"home","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"worried","t":"Susie, every week there's something new. It hurts more than I let people see."},{"sc":"home","n":"Susannah wanted to help. So she had a Bible verse printed in large letters, put it in a frame, and hung it in their bedroom, where he would see it every morning."},{"sc":"home","n":"It said: 'Blessed are ye, when men shall revile you, and persecute you, and shall say all manner of evil against you falsely, for my sake. Rejoice, and be exceeding glad: for great is your reward in heaven.'"},{"sc":"home","w":"susie","e":"smile","t":"Remember, Charles: if they're mocking you for Jesus' sake, heaven calls you blessed."},{"sc":"hall","n":"Spurgeon kept going. He didn't hit back with insults. He just kept preaching Christ. And the crowds kept growing, year after year, for nearly forty years. The 'passing fad' never passed."}],"quote":{"text":"Blessed are ye, when men shall revile you... Rejoice, and be exceeding glad: for great is your reward in heaven.","who":"Matthew 5:11-12, framed by Susannah in their bedroom","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"When people mock your faith","text":"The newspapers mocked Spurgeon cruelly, and it hurt. But a framed verse from his wife reminded him every morning: if you're mocked for Jesus, you're blessed. Don't hit back. Keep going, and let time prove the truth."},"think":"What could you put on your wall or phone screen to encourage you when people criticise your faith?","quiz":[{"q":"What did many newspapers say about Spurgeon?","o":["Cruel things: he was a showman and a passing fad","Only kind things","Nothing at all","That he should be king"],"why":"Cartoons too."},{"q":"How did Charles feel about it?","o":["It hurt more than he let people see","He didn't care at all","He was proud","He laughed"],"why":"He told Susie."},{"q":"What did Susannah do to help?","o":["Framed a Bible verse in their bedroom","Wrote angry letters","Burned the newspapers","Moved house"],"why":"He saw it every morning."},{"q":"Which verse was it?","o":["Matthew 5:11-12","John 3:16","Psalm 23","Genesis 1:1"],"why":"Blessed are ye when men revile you."},{"q":"How long did the 'passing fad' last?","o":["Nearly forty years","One year","Six months","A week"],"why":"The crowds kept growing."}]}}},"cs6":{"challenge":[{"q":"Where did the disaster of 19 October 1856 happen?","o":["Surrey Gardens Music Hall","The Crystal Palace","Waterbeach","Exeter Hall"],"why":"A false alarm caused panic."},{"q":"How old was Spurgeon at the time of the disaster?","o":["Twenty-two","Sixteen","Forty","Nineteen"],"why":"His twins were just a month old."},{"q":"Which verse lifted Spurgeon out of his darkness?","o":["Philippians 2:9","Jeremiah 45:5","Psalm 91:10","1 Peter 2:7"],"why":"A name above every name."},{"q":"What did Spurgeon do about his struggles with depression?","o":["Spoke openly to help others","Kept them secret","Stopped preaching for ever","Pretended they weren't real"],"why":"It's okay to not be okay."},{"q":"What verse did Spurgeon shout to test the Crystal Palace?","o":["Behold the Lamb of God","Look unto me","Jesus wept","Let there be light"],"why":"And a workman heard."},{"q":"How many people heard him at the Crystal Palace on 7 October 1857?","o":["23,654","2,000","236","100,000"],"why":"Without a microphone."},{"q":"From what year were his sermons printed weekly?","o":["1855","1834","1892","1900"],"why":"Sold for a penny."},{"q":"What did Spurgeon start in 1856 with Thomas Medhurst?","o":["The Pastors' College","An orphanage","A newspaper","A school for girls"],"why":"To train preachers."},{"q":"When did Spurgeon die?","o":["31 January 1892","19 June 1834","6 January 1850","1 January 1900"],"why":"Aged fifty-seven."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Behold the ___ of God, which taketh away the sin of the world.\"","o":["Lamb","Lion","King","Son"],"why":"John 1:29."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What was the one simple message Spurgeon preached all his life?","o":["Look to Jesus, and live","Try harder","Be famous","Stay at home"],"why":"From a snowy chapel to the whole world."},"choices":[{"title":"A 'joke' alarm","q":"Your classmates think it would be funny to shout \"Fire!\" in the crowded school hall during assembly.","o":[{"t":"Refuse, tell them how dangerous it is, and warn a teacher if they still plan it.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! A false alarm at the Surrey Gardens cost seven lives."},{"t":"Laugh and join in.","f":"Panic can hurt and even kill people."},{"t":"Stay out of it and say nothing.","f":"Speaking up could prevent real harm."}]},{"title":"When you're very low","q":"For weeks you've felt very sad and heavy. You don't enjoy things any more. You think you should just hide it.","o":[{"t":"Tell a parent, teacher or trusted adult how you're feeling, and keep praying.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Spurgeon was honest about his darkness. Asking for help is brave."},{"t":"Hide it. Christians shouldn't feel like this.","f":"Even Spurgeon felt like this. You don't need to be ashamed."},{"t":"Try to cope completely alone.","f":"You don't have to carry it alone."}]},{"title":"Words that count","q":"You're chatting with a friend who seems worried about something big in her life.","o":[{"t":"Listen, and gently share a Bible verse or tell her you'll pray for her.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! One sentence at the Crystal Palace changed a workman's life."},{"t":"Change the subject to something fun.","f":"Fun is good, but she might need hope too."},{"t":"Tell her it's not a big deal.","f":"Take people's worries seriously."}]},{"title":"Pass it on","q":"You've learned to play the keyboard well. A younger child at church wants to learn.","o":[{"t":"Offer to teach them a little each week.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Spurgeon started the Pastors' College by teaching one student."},{"t":"Say you're too busy.","f":"Passing on skills multiplies blessing."},{"t":"Show off instead of teaching.","f":"Teach to help, not to impress."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"hall","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's how you'll use Spurgeon's story for the rest of your life."},{"sc":"arbour","n":"🌤️ In the dark, remember that Jesus is still Lord. Tell someone. Getting help is brave."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"🏛️ Every word counts. Point people to the Lamb of God."},{"sc":"college","n":"🎓 Pass it on. Teach what you've learned to someone else."},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"👀 Look to Jesus, and live. Then help others look too."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"🔥 Well done! You've finished Spurgeon's Early Years."}],"stops":{"cs6s1":{"story":[{"sc":"hall","n":"By 1856, no church in London could hold the crowds who wanted to hear Spurgeon. So his church rented the brand-new {Surrey Gardens Music Hall}, a huge concert hall."},{"sc":"hall","n":"On Sunday evening, 19 October 1856, about twelve thousand people crammed inside. Thousands more stood outside who couldn't get in."},{"sc":"hall","n":"Charles was only twenty-two. His twin sons were just a month old. He began the service with a hymn, a Bible reading and prayer."},{"sc":"hall","n":"Suddenly, someone in the crowd shouted 'Fire!' Others shouted that the galleries were falling down."},{"sc":"hall","n":"There was no fire, and the galleries were not falling. But thousands of people panicked and rushed for the exits all at once. On the crowded stairs, people were pushed and crushed."},{"sc":"hall","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"shock","t":"Stay calm! Please, stay where you are! There's no danger!"},{"sc":"hall","n":"Charles tried to calm the crowd, but in the noise few could hear him. When it was over, seven people had died, and many more were injured."},{"sc":"night","n":"Charles was so shocked and heartbroken that friends had to help him out of the building. It was the worst night of his life."},{"sc":"night","n":"It's important to know that this was not his fault. People who wanted to cause trouble had shouted the false alarm. But Charles felt the pain of it for the rest of his life."}],"quote":{"text":"Why art thou cast down, O my soul? and why art thou disquieted in me? hope thou in God: for I shall yet praise him.","who":"Psalm 42:5","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"When terrible things happen","text":"A false alarm caused a tragedy at the Surrey Gardens, and the young pastor was crushed with grief. Sometimes terrible things happen that we didn't cause and can't undo. It's right to grieve. And God is near the broken-hearted."},"think":"Why do you think a false alarm caused so much harm? Why is it wrong to spread false alarms or rumours, even as a joke?","quiz":[{"q":"Why did the church rent the Surrey Gardens Music Hall?","o":["No church could hold the crowds","For a concert","For a wedding","It was free"],"why":"About twelve thousand came."},{"q":"When was the night of panic?","o":["19 October 1856","6 January 1850","7 October 1857","8 January 1856"],"why":"A Sunday evening."},{"q":"What caused the panic?","o":["A false shout of 'Fire!'","A real fire","An earthquake","A storm"],"why":"There was no fire."},{"q":"How did Charles feel afterwards?","o":["Shocked and heartbroken","Proud","Calm and happy","Angry with everyone"],"why":"The worst night of his life."},{"q":"What does Psalm 42:5 say to a downcast soul?","o":["Hope thou in God","Give up","Hide away","Forget everything"],"why":"I shall yet praise him."}]},"cs6s2":{"story":[{"sc":"home","n":"After the disaster, Charles went to stay quietly at a friend's house outside London. He was in a deep, dark sadness."},{"sc":"home","n":"For days, he couldn't stop crying. He could hardly pray. He couldn't even bear to read his Bible. He felt as if he would never preach again."},{"sc":"home","w":"susie","e":"worried","t":"Charles, my love. God hasn't left you. Rest. Just rest. I'm here."},{"sc":"arbour","n":"One day, he was walking slowly in the garden. Suddenly a Bible verse came into his mind, as bright as the sun breaking through clouds."},{"sc":"arbour","n":"It was Philippians 2:9: 'Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name.'"},{"sc":"arbour","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"Jesus is still exalted! Whatever happens to me, he is still Lord. It doesn't matter what happens to me, so long as he is lifted high."},{"sc":"arbour","n":"In that moment, the darkness began to lift. Within a couple of weeks, he was back in the pulpit."},{"sc":"hall","n":"Spurgeon suffered from times of deep sadness, or {depression}, for the rest of his life. He never hid it. He spoke openly about it, to comfort others who felt the same."},{"sc":"hall","w":"oldspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"If you're in the dark, don't be ashamed. Many of God's best servants have been there too. Tell someone. And hold on to Jesus."}],"quote":{"text":"Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name.","who":"Philippians 2:9, the verse that lifted Spurgeon","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"It's okay to not be okay","text":"Even the great Spurgeon went through deep darkness and depression. He wasn't ashamed of it, and he talked about it to help others. If you ever feel very low for a long time, tell a trusted adult. Getting help is brave. And Jesus is still Lord in the dark."},"think":"Why do you think it helped people that a famous preacher was honest about his sadness?","quiz":[{"q":"How did Charles feel after the disaster?","o":["A deep, dark sadness","Excited","Bored","Angry"],"why":"He couldn't stop crying."},{"q":"What couldn't he bear to do for days?","o":["Read his Bible","Eat","Walk","Sleep"],"why":"He could hardly pray."},{"q":"Which verse lifted him out of the dark?","o":["Philippians 2:9","John 3:16","Genesis 1:1","Isaiah 45:22"],"why":"Jesus is highly exalted."},{"q":"How soon was he back in the pulpit?","o":["Within a couple of weeks","Ten years later","Never","The next day"],"why":"The darkness began to lift."},{"q":"What did Spurgeon do about his depression?","o":["Spoke openly about it to comfort others","Hid it completely","Pretended it wasn't real","Blamed others"],"why":"It's okay to not be okay."}]},"cs6s3":{"story":[{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"In October 1857, Charles was invited to preach at the {Crystal Palace}, the enormous glass building where he had once sat with Susannah."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"There were no microphones or loudspeakers in those days. So, a day or two before, Charles went to the empty hall to test how far his voice would carry."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"He stood on the platform and called out a Bible verse, loudly, into the huge space:"},{"sc":"crystalpalace","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"big","t":"Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world!"},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"High up in one of the galleries, a workman was busy at his job. He had no idea anyone was there. Suddenly, the words rang out across the hall."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","w":"workman","e":"shock","t":"The Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world? Even my sin?"},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"The words went into his heart like an arrow. He put down his tools, went home, and found peace with God. Years later, Spurgeon heard about it and was overjoyed."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"On 7 October 1857, Charles preached at the Crystal Palace to 23,654 people, without a microphone. It was one of the largest crowds anyone had ever preached to indoors."},{"sc":"crystalpalace","n":"Twenty-three thousand people heard him. But one of the most famous results came from just one sentence, shouted into an empty hall, to one man nobody knew was listening."}],"quote":{"text":"Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world.","who":"John 1:29, called out to test the Crystal Palace","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Every word counts","text":"Spurgeon preached to over twenty-three thousand people. But one sentence spoken to an empty hall changed a workman's life. You never know who is listening, or which words God will use. So speak words that point to Jesus."},"think":"Why do you think God used one sentence in an empty hall so powerfully?","quiz":[{"q":"Why did Charles go to the empty hall before preaching?","o":["To test how far his voice would carry","To practise singing","To sleep","To meet Susannah"],"why":"No microphones then!"},{"q":"What did he call out?","o":["Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world","Hello!","Look unto me","Fire!"],"why":"John 1:29."},{"q":"Who heard him?","o":["A workman in the gallery","The Queen","Nobody","Mr Knill"],"why":"He didn't know anyone was there."},{"q":"How many people came on 7 October 1857?","o":["23,654","236","2,365,400","23"],"why":"Without a microphone!"},{"q":"What is the lesson of the workman?","o":["You never know who is listening, so point to Jesus","Always shout","Don't test halls","Work is boring"],"why":"Every word counts."}]},"cs6s4":{"story":[{"sc":"newsroom","n":"From 1855, one of Spurgeon's sermons was printed every week and sold for a penny. People all over Britain bought them."},{"sc":"newsroom","n":"Soon they were being sent all over the world: to America, Australia, India and Africa. They were translated into many languages."},{"sc":"newsroom","n":"By the end of his life, more than three thousand five hundred of his sermons had been published. They filled over sixty volumes, and they are still read today."},{"sc":"study","n":"Charles also wanted to train other preachers. In 1856, a young man named Thomas Medhurst came to faith through his preaching and wanted to preach too."},{"sc":"study","w":"medhurst","e":"smile","t":"Mr Spurgeon, I want to preach Christ, but I've had very little education. Will you teach me?"},{"sc":"study","w":"youngspurgeon","e":"big","t":"Come to my house every week, Thomas. I'll teach you everything I can."},{"sc":"college","n":"That was the start of the {Pastors' College}. Charles paid for it largely out of his own money. Over the years, it trained hundreds of preachers, many of them poor young men who could never have afforded college."},{"sc":"college","n":"The boy who missed his own college meeting because of a maid's mistake ended up starting a college himself!"}],"quote":{"text":"And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also.","who":"2 Timothy 2:2","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Pass it on","text":"Spurgeon didn't keep his gift to himself. He printed sermons for the world and trained hundreds of preachers. Great leaders multiply themselves by teaching others. What you learn, pass on."},"think":"Spurgeon taught Thomas Medhurst one-to-one at first. Who could you teach or mentor, even in something small?","quiz":[{"q":"How much did a printed sermon cost?","o":["A penny","A pound","Nothing","A gold coin"],"why":"Sold every week from 1855."},{"q":"About how many sermons were published?","o":["More than three thousand five hundred","Ten","One hundred","One"],"why":"Over sixty volumes."},{"q":"Who was the first student of the Pastors' College?","o":["Thomas Medhurst","Mr Vinter","Old Roads","Dr Angus"],"why":"In 1856."},{"q":"Who mostly paid for the college?","o":["Spurgeon, from his own money","The government","The Queen","The students"],"why":"For poor young men."},{"q":"What does 2 Timothy 2:2 tell us to do?","o":["Teach faithful people who will teach others","Keep knowledge secret","Teach only once","Stop learning"],"why":"Pass it on."}]},"cs6s5":{"story":[{"sc":"londonfog","n":"The crowds never stopped growing. So in 1859, the church began building a huge new home: the {Metropolitan Tabernacle}."},{"sc":"mettabernacle","n":"It opened in 1861. It could seat about five thousand people, and around six thousand when it was packed. And it was packed, Sunday after Sunday."},{"sc":"mettabernacle","n":"Spurgeon preached there for thirty-one years. He also started an orphanage for hundreds of children, homes for poor elderly women, and a society to send Christian books around the country."},{"sc":"mettabernacle","w":"oldspurgeon","e":"smile","t":"I have preached many sermons. But my message has always been the same. Look to Jesus, and live."},{"sc":"mettabernacle","n":"He worked so hard that his body grew tired and ill. On 31 January 1892, Charles Haddon Spurgeon died in the south of France, aged fifty-seven."},{"sc":"mettabernacle","n":"Around a hundred thousand people came to see his coffin and his funeral in London. On top of the coffin lay his Bible, open at the verse that had changed his life: 'Look unto me, and be ye saved, all the ends of the earth.'"},{"sc":"snowchapel","n":"Think about it. A snowstorm. An empty pulpit. A thin man with one simple verse. And from that one morning, millions of people around the world heard about Jesus."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"Charles Spurgeon: the boy from the manse, the teenager in the snowy chapel, the boy preacher of the fens, the Prince of Preachers. And his message for you is the same one he heard that day: Look to Jesus, and live."}],"quote":{"text":"Look unto me, and be ye saved, all the ends of the earth.","who":"Isaiah 45:22, the verse on Spurgeon's open Bible at his funeral","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"One message, one life","text":"From a snowy chapel to a tabernacle for thousands, Spurgeon's message never changed: look to Jesus and live. You don't need to be famous to shine. Point people to Jesus, and God can do far more with your life than you can imagine."},"think":"Spurgeon's whole life came from one simple verse. Which Bible verse would you want to sum up your life?","quiz":[{"q":"When did the Metropolitan Tabernacle open?","o":["1861","1834","1850","1892"],"why":"Building began in 1859."},{"q":"About how many people could it hold?","o":["Five to six thousand","Fifty","Five hundred","Fifty thousand"],"why":"And it was packed."},{"q":"How long did Spurgeon preach at the Tabernacle?","o":["Thirty-one years","One year","Five years","Fifty years"],"why":"Week after week."},{"q":"What lay on top of his coffin?","o":["His Bible, open at Isaiah 45:22","A crown","A flag","A newspaper"],"why":"Look unto me, and be ye saved."},{"q":"What does Daniel 12:3 say about those who turn many to righteousness?","o":["They shall shine as the stars for ever","They will be forgotten","They will be rich","They will be tired"],"why":"Shine like stars!"}]}}}});
